          How to Calculate Your Math SAT Score: Predict Your Score Step by Step
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          A complete guide to understanding the Digital SAT math scoring curve, converting raw scores to scaled scores, and using a math SAT calculator to predict your results before test day.
        </p>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-4 text-xs text-slate-400">
          <time datetime="2026-02-17">Updated February 2026</time>
          <span>15 min read</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">

        <p>
          You just finished a practice test and you have your raw numbers in front of you. Now what? How do those correct answers turn into a score between 200 and 800? And more importantly, can you use those results to predict your actual SAT math score on test day?
        </p>
        <p>
          The short answer: yes, you can predict your SAT score with reasonable accuracy. Practice test results are one of the strongest predictors of real test performance, and the math behind the scoring curve is well understood. This guide walks through the entire process — from counting raw scores to applying the adaptive scoring curve — so you know exactly where you stand and what you need to hit your target.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to skip straight to the numbers, plug your practice test results into our <a href="/math-sat-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">math SAT calculator</a> and get an instant estimate.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="sat-math-structure">How the SAT Math Section Is Structured</h2>

        <p>
          Before you can calculate your score, you need to understand what you are calculating. The Digital SAT Math section has a two-module adaptive structure that directly affects how your final score is determined.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Module 1:</strong> 22 questions, 35 minutes. Contains a mix of easy, medium, and hard questions. This is the routing module — your performance here determines everything that follows.</li>
          <li><strong>Module 2:</strong> 22 questions, 35 minutes. Difficulty is set based on your Module 1 results. You will receive either a harder or easier set of questions.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The total is 44 math questions across 70 minutes. Every question counts equally toward your raw score — there is no penalty for guessing, so you should answer every question.
        </p>

        <p>
          The four content domains tested are <strong>Algebra</strong> (about 35% of questions), <strong>Advanced Math</strong> (about 35%), <strong>Problem-Solving and Data Analysis</strong> (about 15%), and <strong>Geometry and Trigonometry</strong> (about 15%). Unlike the old paper SAT, the Digital SAT allows calculator use on every single math question. A built-in <strong>Desmos graphing calculator</strong> is available on screen throughout the section.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="raw-to-scaled">Step 1: Count Your Raw Score</h2>

        <p>
          Your raw math score is the total number of questions you answered correctly across both modules. No points are subtracted for wrong answers or skipped questions. If you got 18 correct in Module 1 and 16 correct in Module 2, your raw score is 34 out of 44.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is straightforward for practice tests where you have the answer key. For the real SAT, the College Board calculates this for you. But when using a <strong>sat score calc</strong> tool to estimate your results, your raw score is the starting point.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-indigo-50 border border-indigo-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-indigo-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">Keep Module Scores Separate</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">When using a math SAT calculator, enter your Module 1 and Module 2 scores separately — not as a single combined number. The calculator needs to know your per-module performance to determine which difficulty path you were on and apply the correct scoring curve.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="adaptive-routing">Step 2: Determine Your Difficulty Path</h2>

        <p>
          This is the part that makes SAT Math scoring different from any standardized test you have taken before. The Digital SAT uses <strong>adaptive module routing</strong>, which means not all test-takers answer the same questions.
        </p>

        <p>
          After you complete Module 1, the system evaluates your performance and assigns you one of two Module 2 difficulty levels:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose my-8">
          <div class="bg-emerald-50 p-6 rounded-xl border border-emerald-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-900 mb-2">Harder Module 2</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-emerald-700 mb-3">
              Triggered when you answer roughly <strong>60-70% or more</strong> of Module 1 questions correctly. This unlocks the full scoring range.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-emerald-700 font-semibold">
              Score ceiling: up to 800
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 p-6 rounded-xl border border-amber-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-900 mb-2">Easier Module 2</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700 mb-3">
              Triggered when you answer below roughly <strong>50-60%</strong> of Module 1 correctly. Your scoring ceiling drops significantly.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700 font-semibold">
              Score ceiling: approximately 600-650
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          This routing mechanism is the single most important factor in your SAT math score. Two students with the same total raw score can receive different scaled scores depending on which Module 2 path they were assigned. A student who got 15 correct in Module 1 and 19 correct in Module 2 (hard path) will score higher than a student who got 10 correct in Module 1 and 24 correct in Module 2 (easy path), even though the second student answered more total questions correctly.
        </p>

        <p>
          When you use our <a href="/" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">SAT score calculator</a>, it automatically detects your likely routing based on your Module 1 accuracy. If you scored above 70% on Module 1, it applies the harder module curve. If below, it uses the easier module curve.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 4 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="scoring-curve">Step 3: Apply the Scoring Curve</h2>

        <p>
          The scoring curve converts your raw score into a scaled score between 200 and 800. The College Board uses a statistical method called <strong>Item Response Theory (IRT)</strong> combined with equating to create this curve. In practice, what matters to you is how many questions you can miss and still hit your target score.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is an estimated scoring curve for the <strong>harder Module 2 path</strong>, based on analysis of official Bluebook practice test data:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">Raw Score (out of 44)</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Questions Missed</th>
