        <p>
          The difference is dramatic. On the hard path, 39 out of 44 correct gives you an estimated 730. On the easy path, the same raw score gives you roughly 600. This is why Module 1 performance is the single most important factor in your SAT math result.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 5 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="predict-sat-score">How to Predict Your SAT Score Before the Real Test</h2>

        <p>
          If you have not taken the SAT yet, you can still predict your SAT score with solid accuracy. Here is a data-driven approach that works.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">Take an Official Practice Test</h3>

        <p>
          Download the <strong>College Board Bluebook app</strong> and take one of the six official practice tests under real testing conditions. That means timed sections, no extra breaks, and no looking at your phone. The Bluebook app automatically scores your test and shows your routing path, making it the most reliable way to estimate your score.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want a second opinion or a more detailed breakdown, enter your per-module raw scores into our <a href="/sat-practice-test-score-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">practice test score calculator</a>. It uses curves matched to each specific Bluebook test form for higher accuracy.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">How Accurate Are Practice Test Predictions?</h3>

        <p>
          Practice test scores are the best predictor of actual SAT performance. Most students score within <strong>30-40 points</strong> of their most recent full-length practice test. The prediction improves when you:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Simulate real conditions</strong> — timed, no distractions, complete both sections</li>
          <li><strong>Use official materials</strong> — Bluebook practice tests use the same adaptive algorithm as the real test</li>
          <li><strong>Average multiple practice tests</strong> — a single test can be an outlier due to luck or fatigue; the average of two or three tests is more reliable</li>
          <li><strong>Account for test-day nerves</strong> — some students score slightly lower on the real test due to anxiety; factor in a small buffer when setting targets</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-emerald-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">Prediction Formula</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">A simple but effective prediction: take the average of your two most recent practice test math scores. If you scored 680 and 720, your predicted score is <strong>700 &plusmn; 30 points</strong>, meaning your likely test-day math score falls between 670 and 730. Use our <a href="/math-sat-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">math SAT calculator</a> to refine this estimate with your exact per-module numbers.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 6 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="score-targets">What Math Score Should You Aim For?</h2>

        <p>
          Your target math score should be based on the schools you are applying to and your intended major. Here is a practical reference:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">Goal</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Target Math Score</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">Context</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">STEM at a Top-20 School</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">770-800</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">MIT, Caltech, and top engineering programs expect near-perfect math</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Ivy League / Top-20</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">740-800</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Competitive range for selective universities regardless of major</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Top-50 University</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">680-750</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Solid for most selective schools</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">State Flagship</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">600-680</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Competitive for admission and scholarships</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Above Average</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">550+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">Above the national median of ~520</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          If you are applying to engineering or computer science programs, math scores carry extra weight. An admissions officer at a top engineering school is more likely to notice a 780 Math / 680 R&W split than a perfectly balanced 730/730. Focus your prep accordingly.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 7 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="improve-math-score">How to Improve Your Math Score</h2>

        <p>
          Knowing where you stand is the first step. Here is a targeted strategy for closing the gap between your current score and your target.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">1. Protect Module 1 at All Costs</h3>

        <p>
          Since Module 1 determines your scoring ceiling, treat it as the highest-stakes 22 questions of the test. Double-check your work. Use every minute of the 35-minute time limit. Getting routed to the harder Module 2 is the prerequisite for any score above 650. If you are currently scoring in the 500-600 range, improving your Module 1 accuracy is the fastest way to see a large score jump.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">2. Master the Desmos Calculator</h3>

        <p>
          The built-in Desmos graphing calculator is available on every math question. Many students underuse it. You can graph equations to find intercepts, solve systems of equations visually, check your algebra by plugging in values, and even use regression tools. Spending a few hours learning Desmos before test day can be worth 20-40 points. Practice using it at <a href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">desmos.com</a> with SAT-style problems.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">3. Focus on Algebra and Advanced Math First</h3>

        <p>
          These two domains account for roughly 70% of all math questions. If your algebra fundamentals are solid (linear equations, systems, inequalities) and you can handle quadratics and polynomials, you have the foundation for a 700+ score. Geometry and trig only appear in about 6-7 questions total — important, but not where you should start your prep unless your algebra is already strong.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">4. Learn to Back-Solve</h3>

        <p>
          On multiple-choice questions, plugging answer choices back into the problem can be faster than solving algebraically, especially on Advanced Math questions with complex expressions. Start with the middle value (choice B or C) — if it is too large, try a smaller option; if too small, try larger. This technique works on a surprising number of questions and eliminates the risk of algebraic errors.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">5. Review Every Mistake</h3>

        <p>
          After each practice test, categorize every wrong answer: was it a conceptual gap (you didn't know how to solve it), a careless error (you knew the method but made a mistake), or a time management issue (you ran out of time)? This tells you exactly what to fix. Conceptual gaps require study. Careless errors require slower, more deliberate checking. Time issues require practicing under timed conditions.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 8 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes When Calculating Your SAT Math Score</h2>

        <p>
          Students frequently make these errors when estimating their scores:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
          <li><strong>Ignoring the module path.</strong> Adding up all correct answers and looking at a single conversion table gives you a misleading number. The hard and easy Module 2 curves produce very different scaled scores from the same raw total. Always use a calculator that accounts for adaptive routing.</li>
          <li><strong>Using old SAT conversion tables.</strong> The Digital SAT (launched in 2024) uses a different scoring model than the pre-2024 paper SAT. Old conversion charts from the 1600-point paper SAT do not apply to the Digital SAT. Make sure any <strong>sat score calc</strong> tool you use is built on Digital SAT data.</li>
          <li><strong>Treating all practice tests as equal.</strong> The six Bluebook practice tests have slightly different curves. Bluebook Test 1 may be slightly more or less forgiving than Test 4 at the top of the scale. For the most accurate estimate, use a <a href="/sat-practice-test-score-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">calculator that matches curves to specific practice tests</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring the confidence range.</strong> A score estimate is not a single number — it is a range. When a math SAT calculator tells you 720, it means approximately 690-750 depending on the specific test form. Plan your study targets with this margin in mind.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Section 9 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="math-vs-total">Math Score vs. Total Score: What Matters More?</h2>

        <p>
          Your math score is half of your total SAT score, but its importance varies by context. For STEM-focused applications, admissions committees pay closer attention to math. Business programs at competitive schools also value strong quantitative scores. For humanities and social science applications, a balanced score or strong R&W score may carry slightly more weight.
        </p>

        <p>
          Regardless of your major, most colleges publish and evaluate total scores. A 1400 total with a 650 Math / 750 R&W is treated the same as a 1400 with 750 Math / 650 R&W for general admissions at most schools. The exception is programs that specifically screen for quantitative ability.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you are trying to maximize your total score efficiently, focus on whichever section has more room to grow. Going from 600 to 700 in math is typically easier than going from 700 to 750 — the first requires closing conceptual gaps, while the second requires eliminating nearly all mistakes.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-amber-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">Superscoring Strategy</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">If your target schools <a href="/can-you-superscore-digital-sat" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">superscore the SAT</a>, you can focus on one section per test sitting. Take the SAT once focusing on R&W prep, then again focusing on math. Your superscore combines the best section scores from all sittings, potentially giving you a composite higher than any single test.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 10 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="using-the-calculator">Putting It All Together: Using the Math SAT Calculator</h2>

        <p>
          Here is the step-by-step process to calculate and predict your math SAT score:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
          <li>
            <strong>Complete a practice test.</strong> Use an official Bluebook practice test for the most reliable data.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Count your raw scores by module.</strong> Note how many you got correct in Math Module 1 (out of 22) and Math Module 2 (out of 22) separately.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Open the <a href="/math-sat-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">math SAT calculator</a>.</strong> Enter your Module 1 and Module 2 correct answers. If you used a specific Bluebook test, select it from the practice test dropdown for a curve matched to that form.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Review your estimated score.</strong> The calculator will show your estimated math section score (200-800), the difficulty path you were routed to, a confidence range, and your approximate percentile.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Compare to your target.</strong> Use the <a href="/blog/what-is-a-good-sat-score" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">score benchmarks guide</a> to see if your estimated score is competitive for your target schools. If not, identify the gap and focus your study plan on closing it.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
          Repeat this process with each practice test you take. Tracking your estimated scores over time shows whether your study plan is working and how close you are to your target.
        </p>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 !mt-6">
          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">How do I calculate my SAT Math score from a practice test?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">Count the number of questions you answered correctly in Math Module 1 (out of 22) and Math Module 2 (out of 22). Your raw score is the total correct answers. Then use a scoring curve — either from the official Bluebook practice test or a <a href="/math-sat-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">math SAT calculator</a> — to convert that raw score into a scaled score between 200 and 800.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Can I predict my SAT score before taking the test?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">Yes. Take a full-length official practice test under timed conditions using the College Board's Bluebook app. Enter your raw scores into a SAT score calculator to get an estimated scaled score. Practice test scores are strong predictors of actual test-day performance, typically within 30-40 points.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">What is the SAT Math scoring curve?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">The scoring curve converts your raw score (number correct) into a scaled score (200-800). It varies by test form and depends on your Module 2 difficulty path. On the harder path, each correct answer is weighted more heavily, allowing scores up to 800. On the easier path, scores are capped around 600-650.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">How many math questions can I miss and still get a 700?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">On the harder Module 2 path, you can typically miss about 5-7 questions out of 44 and still score around 700. On the easier Module 2 path, reaching 700 is extremely difficult since the scoring ceiling is capped around 600-650.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Does getting routed to the easy Module 2 ruin my score?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">It significantly limits your ceiling. If you are routed to the easier Module 2, your Math score will typically cap around 600-650 even if you answer every question in Module 2 correctly. Performing well on Module 1 is critical for accessing the full scoring range.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Is the SAT Math curve the same on every test?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">No. The College Board adjusts curves for each test form through a process called equating. Some forms have slightly more forgiving curves. This is why score estimates include a confidence range of approximately &plusmn;30 points.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

